That's not the least bit surprising.

Notice also that there is no guarantee that a
"<pre>" tag will produce fixed pitch text; the HTML
4.01 language is permissive, not directive:

> The PRE element tells visual user agents that the
> enclosed text is "preformatted". When handling
> preformatted text, visual user agents:

>    * May leave white space intact.
>    * May render text with a fixed-pitch font.
>    * May disable automatic word wrap.
>    * Must not disable bidirectional processing.

http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/struct/text.html#h-9.3.4

Which means that the style of Google Groups output
as seen by the user is entirely browser
implementation dependent as well as browser vendor
specific and browser version dependent. The end
result of that mess is that two human beings may
have quite opposite experiences of what is "normal".

In the case of IE 6.0, that browser is delivered
with default Web page font set to Times New Roman, a
proportional font, and with default Plain text font
set to Courier New.  Since Web page font is used
where _no_ font type choice is indicated, and since
Google's HTML <head> section and <body> tag for news
articles in "normal" mode specify no font type
choice, the parts of the news article outside the
<pre> tags are rendered in Times New Roman, that is,
they are in a proportional font. Since IE 6.0
conforms to the usual expectation of the <pre> tags,
the parts of the news article within those tags are
rendered by default in Courier New, a fixed pitch
font.

Mozilla's default font setting system is richer,
with choices for Proportional, Serif, Sans-serif,
Cursive, Fantasy, and Monospace all able to be
independently set at the same time. The result
with the defaults as delivered (I just downloaded
and installed a Mozilla daily build a couple of
hours ago, so I'm absolutely certain what I have is
the default setup as delivered), however, is exactly
the same, Times New Roman for the proportional font,
Courier New for the monospaced font, and the
proportional font used in preference to the
monospaced font if no choice is expressed on the web
page or its associated style sheets, if any.

That's why I'm fairly sure that what I'm seeing is
very much like what you're seeing, you just aren't
looking carefully enough.

